  <Patch Id="UM5Vqz29l5ReFDWRZcdEAe">
    <Canvas Id="KHAs9yRXA0VgsSGVTYctpq" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="APQqK13sMrYef7es2YMbP3">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="HyEG2YOnjy7c31kNe3aAkW">
        <Canvas Id="O9voHyjtoz11GwSCYmXZ5o" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="S02PgAHcsukga6dUM5vj75" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1560" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Prompt - Paint by numbers.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The world's countries, and one number each: how many people live there. The number IS the paint -&#xD;&#xA;StyleByValue stretches every country's fill between two colours, palest at MIN, deepest at MAX.&#xD;&#xA;Point at a country and its name and number appear above. Drag to pan, wheel to zoom. No tiles, no&#xD;&#xA;network: the countries are the map.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NOW LOOK AT IT. Almost everything is pale. Two countries - China and India, 1.4 billion each -&#xD;&#xA;hold the deep end of the scale so completely that the other 175 crowd the bottom: Brazil's 211&#xD;&#xA;million is a fifteenth of the way up. The map is 'correct' and it says almost nothing. That is not&#xD;&#xA;a bug in the patch; it is the oldest problem in thematic mapping, and it has a name: you mapped a&#xD;&#xA;HEAVY-TAILED number with a LINEAR scale.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;FLIP THE LOG TOGGLE. Same countries, same data - but now the paint follows log10(population): each&#xD;&#xA;factor of ten is one equal step of colour, 140 people to 1.4 billion in seven steps. Structure&#xD;&#xA;appears: continents differ, neighbours differ, the map has something to say everywhere. Nothing&#xD;&#xA;about the data changed. YOU changed, from 'how many more' to 'how many times more' - and the map&#xD;&#xA;tells that to every reader, or lies to every reader, depending on which you picked. A choropleth&#xD;&#xA;is an argument, not a photograph.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE SECOND LIE IS STILL IN THIS MAP, and the toggle does not fix it: these are raw COUNTS painted&#xD;&#xA;onto AREAS. Russia is enormous and mid-coloured; Singapore is invisible and dense. The eye&#xD;&#xA;integrates colour over area, so big empty countries shout and small full ones whisper. The honest&#xD;&#xA;cure is mapping density (people per square km) or an equal-area cartogram - neither is in this&#xD;&#xA;patch, on purpose: fixing it silently would hide that the sin exists. Every choropleth you have&#xD;&#xA;ever seen made this choice, one way or the other.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM. help\Assets\countries-population.geojson - Natural Earth's 1:110m&#xD;&#xA;countries (public domain), thinned to two properties per country: pop and log10_pop. The log&#xD;&#xA;column was precomputed because that is a decision about the MAP, not about the world - the file&#xD;&#xA;admits it by carrying both. Read GeoJSON hands back 177 features; FeatureLayer draws them;&#xD;&#xA;StyleByValue picks the colour per feature by reading the attribute the toggle names.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;MIN AND MAX ARE YOURS TOO. Linear runs 0 to 1.45 billion; log runs 2 to 9.2 (that is 100 people&#xD;&#xA;to 1.6 billion, in powers of ten). Narrow the range and the scale saturates: set MAX to 100&#xD;&#xA;million on linear and suddenly Europe has structure - and everything above MAX wears the same&#xD;&#xA;deepest colour, silently. Classification - equal intervals, quantiles, natural breaks - is the&#xD;&#xA;craft GIS packages put whole dialogs behind; this patch has the two ends of a ramp, and that is&#xD;&#xA;enough to feel why the craft exists.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS. Choropleth (Greek: choros place, plethos multitude) - regions painted by a value.&#xD;&#xA;Attribute: the number attached to a geometry; this file has two. Classification: how values become&#xD;&#xA;classes/colours - the linear/log toggle is its smallest possible version. Normalisation: dividing&#xD;&#xA;by area or total before mapping - the missing cure named above. Graduated symbols: the sibling&#xD;&#xA;that varies SIZE instead of colour (Prompt Live earthquakes does that to dots).&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION. This is data-driven colour, the bread and butter of generative work - a&#xD;&#xA;palette indexed by a signal. Here the signal is a census and the canvas is a planet. Swap the&#xD;&#xA;attribute, swap the ramp ends, clamp the range: every choice changes what the map claims. Make it&#xD;&#xA;lie on purpose, once, to see how easy it is.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT. Prompt Live earthquakes drives SIZE by a value on live data - the same idea, graduated&#xD;&#xA;symbols. Tutorial 09 is where GeoJSON files first arrived. Tutorial 12 asked what happens when&#xD;&#xA;space itself is the value - a field instead of regions; a choropleth is a field forced into&#xD;&#xA;polygons, which is a third lie for you to think about.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Data: Natural Earth 1:110m Admin 0 countries (public domain, naturalearthdata.com). Exercise&#xD;&#xA;shape adapted from FOSS4G GeoAcademy GST 101 Lab 4, 'Displaying Geospatial Data' (CC-BY 3.0).">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Pis1mwl7Oz7kdRyTcwFtOA" Comment="LOG" Bounds="60,0,60,35" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="False">
